export const C1={title:'The Personal Care event and the caregiver role',hook:'Personal Care lets you show what a caregiver does: keep people clean, safe, comfortable and respected. There is no written test. Instead you perform real skills while judges watch. This lesson explains how the event works and what a caregiver’s job is.',objectives:['Explain who can compete in Personal Care and why the Accommodation Form matters.','Describe how the skills round works, including timing and scoring.','Describe the job of a nursing assistant or caregiver.','Use respectful words and actions with every patient.'],sections:[
['Who can compete',[
'Personal Care is one of **four HOSA events for students with an IEP** (Individualized Education Program). The other three are Interviewing Skills, Life Support Skills and Speaking Skills. To compete, you must be classified under the **Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA)**. Students with only a **Section 504** plan are **not** eligible for these four events.',
'Every competitor must complete the **IEP Verification and Accommodation Form**. This form proves you are eligible and lets you ask for **accommodations**—changes that help you show your skills, such as extra explanation of the scenario. Upload the form by **May 15 at midnight Eastern time** for the International Leadership Conference (and follow your state’s deadline too). If the form is missing, you may still compete, but **35 points are taken away**.',
chk('What happens if you forget to submit the Accommodation Form by May 15?',['You can still compete, but 35 points are taken away','You cannot compete at all','Nothing happens','You get extra time'],'Submit it early so you keep every point.')]],
['How the skills round works',[
'There is **one round**: a skills assessment. The approved skills and their times are: **I** handwashing (3 minutes), **II** putting on and taking off a full set of PPE (6), **III** making an unoccupied bed (10), **IV** making an occupied bed (12), **V** admitting a patient (10), **VI** moving a patient with a weak side from bed to chair or wheelchair (6), **VII** measuring vital signs—temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure (12)—and **VIII** ostomy care (10). In Personal Care, **skills are not combined**—each skill is its own task.',
'A judge **reads a short scenario** to you. Your time starts **when the reader finishes**. You must do **every step**, even if you have to say it out loud because equipment is missing. Judges can stop you if something is unsafe. To be recognized as a winner you need **70% or more** of the points. If there is a tie, the skill worth the most points breaks the tie.',
match('Match the skill to its time',[['Handwashing','3 minutes'],['PPE on and off','6 minutes'],['Unoccupied bed','10 minutes'],['Occupied bed','12 minutes'],['Vital signs','12 minutes'],['Ostomy care','10 minutes']])]],
['What to bring and wear',[
'Bring a **photo ID**, a **watch with a second hand** (not a smartwatch) for vital signs and **barrier devices**: non-latex gloves, a gown, goggles or safety glasses and a mask. HOSA gives you a **gait belt**, but you may bring your own. Wear clothing for the job, such as scrubs and closed-toe shoes. You can earn **bonus points for proper dress**.',
tip('Make a packing list a week before the conference. Check off ID, watch, gloves, gown, goggles and mask the night before.')]],
['What a caregiver does',[
'A **nursing assistant** (also called a caregiver or aide) helps people with daily care. This includes bathing, dressing, eating, using the toilet, moving safely, making beds and measuring **vital signs**. Caregivers work in **nursing homes**, **hospitals** and **homes**. They follow the **care plan** written by a nurse and tell the nurse about any changes they notice.',
'Caregivers do **not** give medicine, decide what is wrong with a patient or do tasks they were not trained to do. If someone asks you to do a task you were not taught, say kindly that you cannot and tell the nurse. The Bureau of Labor Statistics expects more jobs for nursing assistants as people live longer and need more care.',
sort('Is this a caregiver task?',['Yes, a caregiver does this','No, a caregiver does not do this'],[['Making a bed',0],['Measuring blood pressure',0],['Helping a patient walk',0],['Giving pills',1],['Telling the patient what disease they have',1],['Telling the nurse about a change',0]])]],
['Respect and patient rights',[
'Every patient has **rights**. They have the right to **privacy**, to be treated with **dignity**, to make **choices** and to **say no** to care. Show respect in every skill: **knock** before you enter, **introduce yourself**, **call the patient by name**, **explain** what you are going to do, **close the curtain** and **keep the patient covered**. Keep what you learn about a patient **private**.',
'Good communication is part of your score. Almost every rating sheet gives points for “appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication.” Speak clearly and kindly, look at the patient, smile and listen. When you finish, ask, “Is there anything else I can do for you?”',
flip('Respectful words and actions',[['Knock','Wait before you enter the room'],['Introduce yourself','“Hi, my name is Jordan. I will be your caregiver.”'],['Explain','“I am going to take your temperature now.”'],['Privacy','Close the curtain and keep the patient covered'],['Choice','“Would you like the blue shirt or the green one?”'],['Finish kindly','“Is there anything else I can do for you?”']])]],
['Practicing for success',[
'Practice each skill **many times** with a checklist. Say the steps out loud while you do them. Ask a teacher or classmate to time you and to mark any step you miss. Many steps repeat in every skill: **wash hands**, **greet**, **identify**, **explain**, **give privacy**, **use gloves when needed**, **wash hands at the end** and **report and document**. Learning these “bookend” steps first makes every skill easier.',
order('Steps that start most skills',['Gather supplies','Wash hands','Greet the patient and say your name','Identify the patient','Explain what you will do','Give privacy']),
kase('Before you start',{a:{text:'The judge finishes reading the scenario. You feel nervous. What should you do first?',choices:[['Take a breath, then wash your hands and gather supplies','b','Correct. Start with the first steps you practiced.',true],['Start without washing your hands to save time','b','Hand hygiene is always the first step.',false]]},b:{text:'You realize there is no thermometer on the table. What should you do?',choices:[['Say the step out loud to the judge and keep going','c','Correct. You can simulate or verbalize steps when equipment is missing.',true],['Stop the skill','c','Keep going and say each step.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish every step in order and thank the patient. Great job!',end:true}})]],
["Observing, reporting and privacy",["Caregivers spend more time with patients than almost anyone else, so they often notice changes first. **Observe** with your senses: what you **see** (a rash, swelling, a pale face), **hear** (coughing, moaning), **smell** (a strange odor) and **feel** (hot or cold skin). Some information is **objective**—you can see or measure it, such as a pulse of 112. Some is **subjective**—only the patient can tell you, such as “My head hurts.” Report both to the nurse, and use the patient’s own words for subjective information.","Report **right away** if a patient has trouble breathing, chest pain, a fall, bleeding, sudden confusion or very unusual vital signs. Other changes can be reported at the end of a task. Patient information is **private**. A U.S. law called **HIPAA** protects health information. Do not talk about patients in hallways, elevators or on social media, and share information only with the care team.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Objective or subjective?","buckets":["Objective (seen or measured)","Subjective (the patient tells you)"],"items":[["Pulse of 112",0],["“I feel sick to my stomach”",1],["A red area on the heel",0],["“My back hurts”",1],["Temperature of 101 °F",0],["“I feel dizzy”",1]]},{"t":"check","q":"Where is it okay to talk about a patient’s care?","options":["With the care team in a private place","In the elevator","On social media","With your friends at lunch"],"why":"HIPAA protects patient privacy."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges score each skill with the official rating sheet. Every step is worth points, so a missed step costs points even if the rest is perfect. Judges watch for safety, infection control and respect. They listen for clear communication with the patient and with the “nurse,” who is often the judge. If you make a mistake, say so calmly, fix it and keep going—judges can only score what you do and say. Speak loudly enough to be heard, especially when you say a step out loud because the equipment is not there. Read every rating sheet in the guidelines before you practice."}]]
],takeaways:['Personal Care is for students classified under IDEA; submit the Accommodation Form by May 15 or lose 35 points.','One skills round; eight skills from 3 to 12 minutes; skills are not combined; 70% to be recognized.','Caregivers help with daily care, follow the care plan and report to the nurse; always knock, introduce, identify, explain and give privacy.'],terms:[['IEP','Individualized Education Program for a student with a disability.'],['Accommodation','A change that helps you show your skills.'],['Care plan','The nurse’s plan for each patient’s care.']],mcqs:[q('Who can compete in Personal Care?','Students classified under IDEA','Students with only a Section 504 plan','Any HOSA member','Teachers','It is one of four IEP events.'),q('What is the penalty for not submitting the Accommodation Form?','35 points','5 points','No penalty','Disqualification','You may still compete.'),q('When does the timer start?','When the reader finishes reading the scenario','When you enter the building','After you wash your hands','When you finish','Listen carefully to the scenario.'),q('What score do you need to be recognized as a winner?','70% or higher','50%','100%','90%','On the skills.'),q('What should you bring for vital signs?','A watch with a second hand','A smartwatch','A phone timer','Nothing','Smartwatches are not allowed.'),q('Which task does a caregiver NOT do?','Give medicine','Make beds','Measure vital signs','Help patients walk','Tell the nurse instead.'),q('What should you do before entering a patient’s room?','Knock','Turn off the lights','Open the window','Take off your gloves','Knocking respects privacy.'),q('Are Personal Care skills combined into one scenario?','No, each skill is separate','Yes, always','Only on Tuesdays','Only for vital signs','Each skill stands alone.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how the Personal Care event works and describe three ways a caregiver shows respect to a patient during care.',rubric:[['Eligibility and form',['iep','idea','accommodation','may 15','35']],['How the round works',['skill','scenario','time','70','every step','verbal']],['Respect and rights',['knock','privacy','dignity','choice','name','explain']],['Caregiver role',['nurse','care plan','report','daily care','medicine']]],model:'Personal Care is a skills event for students classified under IDEA. Each competitor submits the Accommodation Form by May 15, or 35 points are taken away. A judge reads a scenario, and the timer starts when the reading ends; I must do every step of the skill, saying steps out loud if equipment is missing, and I need 70% of the points to be recognized. As a caregiver, I help patients with daily care such as bathing, eating, moving and vital signs, follow the nurse’s care plan and report changes to the nurse, but I do not give medicine. I show respect by knocking before I enter and introducing myself, by explaining what I will do and calling the patient by name, and by protecting privacy—closing the curtain, keeping the patient covered and letting the patient make choices.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD,IDEA,CMS_RIGHTS,BLS_NA]};

export const C2={title:'Handwashing (Skill I)',hook:'Germs are too small to see, but they travel on hands from one person to another. Washing your hands the right way is the best way to stop them. Skill I has 21 steps, and you have 3 minutes.',objectives:['Explain why handwashing stops the spread of germs.','Know when to wash hands and when hand rub is okay.','Perform every step of HOSA Skill I in order.','Avoid common mistakes that make hands dirty again.'],sections:[
['Why handwashing matters',[
'Germs such as bacteria and viruses can cause infections. In hospitals and nursing homes, patients are often sick or weak, so an infection can make them very ill. Germs move from person to person on **hands**, on **equipment** and through the **air**. Clean hands break this chain. That is why **every** Personal Care skill starts and ends with hand hygiene.',
'There are two ways to clean hands. **Soap and water** is needed when hands **look dirty**, after using the bathroom, before eating and after caring for some infections such as *C. difficile*. **Alcohol hand rub** works well when hands do **not** look dirty. Skill I tests washing with **soap and water**.',
sort('Soap and water or hand rub?',['Use soap and water','Hand rub is okay'],[['Hands look dirty',0],['After using the bathroom',0],['Before eating',0],['Before taking a pulse (hands look clean)',1],['After leaving a room (hands look clean)',1]])]],
['Getting ready: Skill I steps 1–4',[
'**1.** Stand back from the sink so your clothes do not touch it. **2.** Take off rings. Use a **paper towel** to push your watch or long sleeves up above your wrists. **3.** Turn the faucet on **with a paper towel**, make the water **warm**, then throw that towel away. **4.** Wet your hands, wrists and the skin **1–2 inches above your wrists**. Keep your **fingertips pointing down** so dirty water runs off your fingers, not up your arms.',
tip('Practice saying “fingertips down” every time you put your hands under the water.')]],
['Scrubbing every part: steps 5–17',[
'**5.** Take your hands out of the water and use soap to make a **thick lather**. **6.** Rub your palms together in a **circle, counterclockwise**. **7.** Push the fingers of your right hand between the fingers of your left hand and rub up and down. **8.** Do the same with the left hand. **9.** **Lace your fingers** together and rub palms side to side. **10.** Bend your fingers and **hook them together** so the backs of your fingers rub the other palm.',
'**11.** Clean **under your fingernails** by rubbing them on the other palm and around the tops of the nails. **12.** Switch hands. **13.** Hold your **left thumb** in your right palm and rub in a counterclockwise circle. **14.** Switch hands. **15.** Put the **fingertips** of your right hand in the middle of your left palm and rub in a counterclockwise circle. **16.** Switch hands. **17.** Wash for **at least 20 seconds**—about as long as singing “Happy Birthday” twice.',
order('Scrubbing order',['Make a thick lather','Rub palms in counterclockwise circles','Rub between fingers on each hand','Lace fingers and rub palms','Hook fingers to scrub the backs','Scrub under and around nails','Scrub each thumb','Scrub fingertips in the opposite palm'])]],
['Rinsing and drying: steps 18–21',[
'**18.** Rinse your hands and wrists under running water with **fingertips down**. **19.** Dry your hands with a **clean paper towel**, starting with the **hands** and then the **wrists**. Do not shake your hands and do not touch the towel dispenser. Use more towels if you need them. **20.** Use a **clean, dry paper towel** to **turn off the faucet**. **21.** Throw the towel away **without touching the trash can**.',
'Why use a new towel for the faucet? The faucet handle is dirty because you touched it at the start. A clean towel keeps your clean hands from touching it again.',
order('Finishing Skill I',['Rinse with fingertips down','Dry hands first, then wrists','Use a clean dry towel to turn off the faucet','Throw the towel away without touching the trash can'])]],
['Common mistakes',[
'Judges often see these mistakes: touching the sink with clothes, turning the faucet on or off with bare hands, fingertips pointing up while rinsing, scrubbing for less than 20 seconds, forgetting thumbs or nails, shaking hands dry and touching the trash can lid. Practice slowly at first, then build speed. You have **3 minutes**, which is enough time when you follow the steps calmly.',
kase('Check your handwashing',{a:{text:'You finish rinsing and reach for the faucet handle with your clean hand. What should you do instead?',choices:[['Dry your hands, then use a clean dry paper towel to turn off the faucet','b','Correct. Clean hands should not touch the faucet.',true],['Turn it off with your hand quickly','b','That puts germs back on your hands.',false]]},b:{text:'You dropped your paper towel on the floor. What now?',choices:[['Get a new clean towel','c','Correct. Anything on the floor is dirty.',true],['Pick it up and use it','c','The floor is dirty.',false]]},c:{text:'You throw the towel away without touching the can. Skill complete!',end:true}})]],
['Practice plan',[
'Use a checklist with all 21 steps. Practice three times a day for a week. First, read each step aloud while you do it. Next, do it without reading and have a partner check your list. Last, have a partner time you. Remember: **stand back**, **towel on**, **fingertips down**, **20 seconds**, **hands then wrists**, **towel off**.',
flip('Memory cues',[['Start','Stand back and remove rings'],['Faucet on','With a paper towel; water warm'],['Fingertips','Always point down'],['Scrub time','At least 20 seconds'],['Drying','Hands first, then wrists'],['Faucet off','With a clean, dry paper towel']])]],
["The chain of infection and standard precautions",["Germs spread through a **chain of infection**: a **germ**, a place where it lives (such as a sick person), a **way out** (like a cough or blood), a **way it travels** (hands, objects, air), a **way in** (mouth, nose, broken skin) and a **person who can get sick**. Breaking **any link** stops the infection. Handwashing breaks the “way it travels” link, which is why it is the most important skill.","**Standard precautions** are the basic rules caregivers use with **every** patient, every time: clean your hands, wear gloves and other PPE when you might touch blood or body fluids, clean equipment between patients, handle dirty linen carefully and put sharps and waste in the right containers. You cannot tell who has an infection just by looking, so treat everyone the same way.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Links in the chain of infection","steps":["Germ","Place where it lives","Way out","Way it travels","Way in","Person who can get sick"]},{"t":"check","q":"Why are standard precautions used for every patient?","options":["You cannot tell who has germs just by looking","Only to save time","Only for sick patients","Because the judge says so"],"why":"Treat all blood and body fluids as if they could spread infection."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges watch your hands the whole time. They check that you stand back from the sink, use a paper towel on the faucet at the start and the end, keep your fingertips down, scrub every part for at least 20 seconds and dry hands before wrists. They also notice if you touch the sink, the towel dispenser or the trash can, because that puts germs back on clean hands. Moving calmly and in order looks more professional than rushing. Many competitors lose points on the faucet steps, so practice them until they feel automatic."}]]
],takeaways:['Handwashing stops germs from spreading; use soap and water when hands look dirty.','Turn the faucet on and off with paper towels; keep fingertips down; scrub at least 20 seconds, including nails, thumbs and fingertips.','Dry hands then wrists, do not shake or touch the dispenser, and throw away the towel without touching the can.'],terms:[['Germs','Tiny living things that can cause infection.'],['Lather','Soap bubbles that help remove germs.'],['Hand hygiene','Cleaning hands with soap and water or hand rub.']],mcqs:[q('How long should you scrub your hands?','At least 20 seconds','5 seconds','2 minutes only if dirty','No time limit','About two “Happy Birthday” songs.'),q('Which way should your fingertips point when you rinse?','Down','Up','Sideways','It does not matter','Water flows off the fingertips.'),q('How do you turn on the faucet?','With a paper towel','With your bare hand','With your elbow only','With your foot','Then throw the towel away.'),q('What do you dry first?','Your hands','Your wrists','Your arms','Your face','Then the wrists.'),q('How do you turn off the faucet?','With a clean, dry paper towel','With wet hands','With the towel you dried with','By kicking it','Keep hands clean.'),q('Which water temperature is used?','Warm','Very hot','Ice cold','It does not matter','Warm water is comfortable.'),q('What should you do with rings?','Take them off before washing','Leave them on','Put them in your pocket after washing','Wash them separately later','Germs hide under rings.'),q('How much time do you have for Skill I?','3 minutes','10 minutes','1 minute','6 minutes','Enough if you follow the steps calmly.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to wash your hands following HOSA Skill I, from getting ready to throwing away the last paper towel.',rubric:[['Getting ready',['stand back','ring','paper towel','faucet','warm']],['Wetting and lather',['fingertips down','wrist','1-2 inches','lather']],['Scrubbing',['palms','between','nails','thumb','fingertips','20 seconds','counterclockwise']],['Rinse and dry',['rinse','hands','wrists','clean paper towel','turn off','trash']]],model:'I stand back from the sink so my clothes do not touch it, take off my rings and use a paper towel to push my watch and sleeves up. I turn on the faucet with a paper towel, make the water warm and throw the towel away. I wet my hands, wrists and the skin 1–2 inches above my wrists with my fingertips pointing down, then take my hands out of the water and make a thick lather with soap. I rub my palms in counterclockwise circles, scrub between my fingers on each hand, lace my fingers, hook my fingers to clean the backs, scrub under and around my nails, scrub each thumb and rub my fingertips in the opposite palm, washing for at least 20 seconds. I rinse with my fingertips down, dry my hands first and then my wrists with a clean paper towel without shaking my hands or touching the dispenser, turn off the faucet with a clean, dry paper towel and throw the towel away without touching the trash can.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HANDS,CDC_HH]};

export const C3={title:'Putting on and taking off PPE (Skill II)',hook:'PPE means personal protective equipment: a gown, a mask, goggles and gloves. PPE protects you and your patients from germs. Putting it on and taking it off in the right order is Skill II, and you have 6 minutes.',objectives:['Name each piece of PPE and what it protects.','Put on a gown, mask, goggles and gloves in the correct order.','Take off PPE in the correct order without touching the dirty outside.','Explain why order matters.'],sections:[
['What PPE does',[
'PPE makes a **barrier** between you and germs. The **gown** protects your clothes and skin. The **mask** covers your nose and mouth. **Goggles** protect your eyes from splashes. **Gloves** protect your hands. Caregivers wear PPE when they might touch blood, body fluids, broken skin or a patient with an infection that spreads easily.',
'The **outside** of PPE becomes dirty during care. The **inside** stays clean. When you take PPE off, you try to touch only the clean parts so germs do not get on your skin.',
"Gloves are single-use. Choose the right size so they do not tear or slide off, and use **non-latex** gloves because some patients and workers are allergic to latex. Never wash gloves or use hand rub on them, and never touch your face, phone or pen while wearing dirty gloves. PPE protects you only when it is worn correctly, taken off in the right order and followed by hand hygiene—gloves can have tiny holes you cannot see.",
match('Match the PPE to what it protects',[['Gown','Clothes and skin'],['Mask','Nose and mouth'],['Goggles','Eyes'],['Gloves','Hands'],['Inside of PPE','The clean side'],['Outside of PPE','The dirty side after care']])]],
['Putting on the gown and mask',[
'First, **gather your supplies** and **wash your hands** (or use hand rub). **Gown:** hold it in front of you and let it **unfold without shaking it**. Face the **back opening** and put your **arms through the sleeves**. **Tie the neck**. Reach behind and pull the gown so it **covers all your clothes**, then **tie the waist**.',
'**Mask:** pick it up by the **top strings or elastic**—do not touch the inside. Pull the elastic over your head or **tie the top strings first, then the bottom strings**. **Pinch the metal strip** so it fits tightly around your nose. Make sure it fits **snugly around your face and below your chin**.',
order('Gown and mask',['Wash hands','Let the gown unfold without shaking','Arms through the sleeves','Tie the neck','Cover all clothes and tie the waist','Pick up the mask by the strings','Tie top strings, then bottom strings','Pinch the nose strip and fit under the chin'])]],
['Goggles and gloves',[
'**Goggles:** place them over your eyes (or over your glasses) so they **fit snugly**. **Gloves:** if you are **right-handed**, put a glove on your **left hand first** (if left-handed, the right hand first). Use the gloved hand to help slide your other hand into the second glove. **Lace your fingers** to smooth out the folds. **Check the gloves** for tears, holes, cracks or spots and change them if needed. Pull the glove **cuffs over the gown sleeves** so no skin shows at your wrists.',
tip('Say the order out loud while you practice: “Gown, mask, goggles, gloves.”'),
chk('Where do the glove cuffs go?',['Over the sleeves of the gown','Under the sleeves','Up to the elbow under the gown','It does not matter'],'Covering the sleeves leaves no gap at the wrist.')]],
['Taking off gloves',[
'When the judge says, “Skill is complete, remove PPE,” take off the **gloves first** because they are the **dirtiest**. With one gloved hand, grab the **outside of the other glove at the palm** and pull it off. **Hold that glove** in your gloved hand. Slide **two fingers of your bare hand under the cuff** of the other glove—touching only the inside. Pull down so the glove turns **inside out over the first glove**. Drop both gloves in the trash.',
order('Remove gloves',['Grab the outside of one glove at the palm','Pull it off and hold it in the gloved hand','Slide two bare fingers under the other cuff','Turn the glove inside out over the first','Drop both gloves in the trash'])]],
['Taking off goggles, gown and mask',[
'Next, **remove the goggles**. Then **untie the gown** at the neck and waist. Take off the gown by **rolling the dirty side in**, touching only the inside and holding it **away from your body**. Put it in the trash or laundry. Then **untie the mask** and remove it **without touching the front**, and throw it away. Finally, **wash your hands** or use hand rub.',
'The order for taking off is: **gloves, goggles, gown, mask, hand hygiene**. The mask comes off last because your hands should be as clean as possible before you touch the strings near your face.',
order('Remove PPE in order',['Gloves','Goggles','Gown (dirty side rolled in)','Mask (touch only the strings)','Wash hands'])]],
['Practice and check yourself',[
kase('Fix the mistake',{a:{text:'You start taking off PPE by untying your mask first. Is that correct?',choices:[['No—gloves come off first because they are the dirtiest','b','Correct. Gloves, goggles, gown, mask, then wash hands.',true],['Yes, the mask is always first','b','The mask is removed last.',false]]},b:{text:'While taking off the gown, it touches your shirt. What should you have done?',choices:[['Roll the dirty side in and hold it away from your body','c','Correct. Keep the dirty side away from you.',true],['Shake the gown off quickly','c','Shaking spreads germs.',false]]},c:{text:'You practice again: gloves, goggles, gown rolled in, mask by the strings, then wash hands. Perfect!',end:true}}),
flip('PPE order cards',[['Putting on','Gown, mask, goggles, gloves'],['Taking off','Gloves, goggles, gown, mask'],['Last step','Wash your hands'],['Mask strings','Top first, then bottom'],['Glove cuffs','Over the gown sleeves'],['Gown removal','Roll the dirty side in']])]],
["Choosing the right PPE",["Different germs spread in different ways, so the nurse may post a sign on the door asking for **transmission-based precautions**. **Contact precautions** are for germs that spread by touch, such as MRSA or *C. difficile*; wear a **gown and gloves**. **Droplet precautions** are for germs in large droplets from coughs and sneezes, such as the flu; wear a **mask**, and goggles if splashes are likely. **Airborne precautions** are for tiny germs that float in the air, such as tuberculosis or measles; staff wear a special fitted **N95 respirator**, and the door stays closed.","Always read the sign on the door **before** you go in. Put on PPE **outside** the room and take most of it off **at the door** before leaving (follow facility policy). Change gloves when they get dirty, and **never** wear the same PPE from one patient to another. In Skill II you put on a full set—gown, mask, goggles and gloves—so you practice every piece.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Which precaution?","buckets":["Contact (gown and gloves)","Droplet (mask)","Airborne (N95, door closed)"],"items":[["MRSA wound",0],["Flu",1],["Tuberculosis",2],["C. difficile diarrhea",0],["Measles",2],["Whooping cough",1]]},{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"PPE words","pairs":[["Contact precautions","Spread by touch"],["Droplet precautions","Spread by coughs and sneezes"],["Airborne precautions","Tiny germs that float in the air"],["N95 respirator","Special fitted mask for airborne germs"],["Door sign","Read it before you enter"],["Standard precautions","Used with every patient"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges look for the correct order both times: gown, mask, goggles, gloves to put on, then gloves, goggles, gown, mask and hand hygiene to take off. They watch whether you touch the outside of dirty PPE with bare skin, whether the gown covers all your clothes, whether the mask fits snugly under your chin and whether the glove cuffs cover the gown sleeves. They also check that you looked at the gloves for tears. If a glove rips, take it off safely, clean your hands and put on a new one. Say each step quietly to yourself to stay on track."}]]
],takeaways:['PPE (gown, mask, goggles, gloves) blocks germs; the outside is dirty and the inside is clean.','Put on: wash hands, gown (neck then waist), mask (top strings first, nose strip), goggles, gloves over the gown cuffs.','Take off: gloves, goggles, gown rolled dirty side in, mask by the strings, then wash hands.'],terms:[['PPE','Personal protective equipment such as gowns, masks, goggles and gloves.'],['Barrier','Something that blocks germs.'],['Contaminated','Dirty with germs.']],mcqs:[q('Which PPE do you put on first?','Gown','Gloves','Mask','Goggles','Gloves go on last.'),q('Which PPE do you take off first?','Gloves','Mask','Gown','Goggles','They are the dirtiest.'),q('How do you tie the mask strings?','Top strings first, then bottom','Bottom first','Only the bottom','You do not tie them','Then pinch the nose strip.'),q('How should you open the gown?','Let it unfold without shaking it','Shake it hard','Turn it inside out','Fold it in half','Shaking spreads germs.'),q('If you are right-handed, which hand gets the first glove?','Left hand','Right hand','Either','Both at the same time','Reverse if left-handed.'),q('How do you take off the gown?','Roll the dirty side in and hold it away from your body','Pull it over your head','Shake it off','Throw it on the floor','Touch only the inside.'),q('What is the very last step of Skill II?','Wash your hands or use hand rub','Remove the gown','Remove the gloves','Put on new gloves','Clean hands finish every skill.'),q('Gloves should be checked for:','Tears, holes, cracks or spots','Color only','Size only','Brand','Replace damaged gloves.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to put on and take off a full set of PPE following HOSA Skill II.',rubric:[['Putting on gown and mask',['gown','unfold','sleeves','neck','waist','mask','top strings','nose']],['Goggles and gloves',['goggles','snug','left hand','second glove','check','cuffs','sleeves']],['Removing gloves and goggles',['gloves first','palm','inside out','two fingers','goggles']],['Removing gown and mask',['gown','roll','dirty side in','away','mask','strings','wash hands']]],model:'I gather supplies and wash my hands. I hold the gown in front of me and let it unfold without shaking it, face the back opening, put my arms through the sleeves, tie the neck and pull the gown to cover all my clothes before tying the waist. I pick up the mask by the strings, tie the top strings first and then the bottom, pinch the nose strip and fit it under my chin. I put on goggles so they fit snugly. Because I am right-handed, I put a glove on my left hand first, use it to help put on the second glove, lace my fingers to smooth them, check for tears and pull the cuffs over the gown sleeves. To take PPE off, I remove the gloves first by grabbing the outside of one at the palm, holding it in my gloved hand and sliding two bare fingers under the other cuff to turn it inside out, then throw them away. I remove the goggles, untie the gown and roll it dirty side in while holding it away from my body, untie the mask and remove it without touching the front, and wash my hands.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_ISO,CDC_HH]};

export const C4={title:'Making an unoccupied bed (Skill III)',hook:'A clean, smooth bed helps patients rest and keeps their skin healthy. An unoccupied bed is one with no one in it. Skill III has 24 steps and 10 minutes. The secret is working on one side until it is done.',objectives:['Explain why clean, smooth linens matter.','Handle dirty linen safely.','Make an unoccupied bed following HOSA Skill III.','Leave the room safe for the patient.'],sections:[
['Why bedmaking matters',[
'Wrinkles, crumbs and wet sheets can hurt a patient’s skin and cause **pressure injuries** (bed sores). Dirty linens can also spread germs. A neat bed with tight, smooth sheets is safer and more comfortable. Caregivers change beds often—when linens are dirty or wet and on a regular schedule.',
'Two rules keep germs away: **hold linens away from your uniform**, and **never shake** linens. Put dirty linen straight into the **laundry hamper**, never on the floor.',
sort('Safe or not safe?',['Safe','Not safe'],[['Rolling dirty linen inward',0],['Shaking a sheet to get crumbs off',1],['Holding linens away from your uniform',0],['Putting dirty linen on the floor',1],['Putting dirty linen in the hamper',0]])]],
['Getting ready: steps 1–8',[
'**1.** Gather supplies. **2.** Wash your hands and put on gloves if needed. **3.** Put the linens nearby **in the order you will use them**. **4.** **Lock the bed wheels** and raise the bed to a comfortable working height (this protects your back). **5.** Remove dirty linens by **rolling the edges inward** so they do not touch your scrubs. **6.** Put them in the **laundry hamper**. **7.** **Work on one side** of the bed until that side is finished—this saves time. **8.** Take off the pillows and put the pillowcases in the hamper.',
order('Linen order (top of the pile is used first)',['Fitted bottom sheet','Drawsheet or pad','Top sheet','Blanket or spread','Pillowcase'])]],
['Bottom sheet and drawsheet: steps 9–10',[
'**9.** Put on the **fitted bottom sheet**. Pull the corners **tight and smooth** over the mattress corners. **10.** Place the **drawsheet** (or an incontinence pad) in the **center of the bed** and tuck it under the mattress so it is **tight and smooth**. A drawsheet helps caregivers move patients and protects the bottom sheet.',
tip('Run your hand over the sheets. If you feel a wrinkle, pull it smooth before you move on.')]],
['Top sheet, blanket and corners: steps 11–15',[
'**11.** Unfold the **top sheet** with the **wrong side up** (seams facing up). The **hem is even with the top edge of the mattress**. Use the **center fold** to center it. **12.** Place the **blanket or spread** on the bed, using its center fold to center it. **13.** Tuck the top sheet and blanket **under the mattress at the foot** of the bed. **14.** Make a **mitered corner** at the foot—a neat, folded corner like wrapping a present—and tuck in the sides. **15.** Fold the top sheet back over the blanket to make a **6-inch cuff**. The cuff looks neat and keeps the blanket from rubbing the patient’s face.',
order('Top layers',['Top sheet wrong side up, hem even with the mattress top','Center the blanket','Tuck both at the foot','Make a mitered corner','Fold a 6-inch cuff'])]],
['Pillows and safety: steps 16–24',[
'**16.** Put the pillows into the pillowcases. **17.** Place the pillows at the head of the bed with the **open end away from the door**. **18.** Check that the **wheels are locked** and **lower the bed to its lowest position**. **19.** Take off gloves if you wore them and wash your hands. **20.** **Place the call light on the bed**. **21.** Put back anything you moved. **22.** Do a **safety check** before leaving. **23.** Wash your hands. **24.** Follow standard precautions the whole time.',
'Why lower the bed and place the call light? A low bed prevents falls, and the call light lets the patient call for help.',
kase('Finishing up',{a:{text:'You finished the pillows. The bed is still high. What do you do?',choices:[['Check the wheels are locked and lower the bed to its lowest position','b','Correct. A low bed is safer.',true],['Leave it high so the next caregiver can use it','b','A high bed can cause falls.',false]]},b:{text:'Where should the call light go?',choices:[['On the bed','c','Correct. The patient must be able to reach it.',true],['In the drawer','c','The patient could not reach it.',false]]},c:{text:'You put things back, do a safety check and wash your hands. The bed is ready!',end:true}})]],
['Practice plan',[
'Practice with a real bed if you can. Time yourself—10 minutes goes fast, so working on one side at a time really helps. Remember the key words: **lock and raise**, **roll inward**, **one side**, **wrong side up**, **miter**, **6-inch cuff**, **open end away**, **lowest position**, **call light**.',
flip('Key words',[['Lock and raise','Wheels locked, bed at working height'],['Roll inward','Dirty linen never touches you'],['One side','Finish one side before the other'],['Wrong side up','Top sheet seams face up'],['6-inch cuff','Top sheet folded over the blanket'],['Lowest position','Then call light on the bed']])]],
["Protecting the skin from pressure injuries",["A **pressure injury** (bed sore) happens when skin and the tissue under it are squeezed for too long, usually over a **bony area** such as the **heels, hips, tailbone, elbows, shoulder blades and back of the head**. Blood cannot reach the skin, and it starts to break down. People who cannot move by themselves, who are thin, who have poor nutrition or who are often wet from sweat or incontinence are at the highest risk.","Caregivers prevent pressure injuries by **turning and repositioning** patients at least **every 2 hours** (or as the care plan says), keeping linens **clean, dry and wrinkle-free**, using pillows to keep bony areas from touching, keeping heels off the bed, and checking the skin during care. Report any **red area that does not fade**, an open spot, a blister or skin that feels warm or hard. Your smooth, tight bed linens are part of skin care.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Helps or harms the skin?","buckets":["Helps prevent pressure injuries","Can cause pressure injuries"],"items":[["Repositioning every 2 hours",0],["Wrinkled, damp sheets",1],["Pillow between the knees",0],["Crumbs in the bed",1],["Heels resting on the mattress all day",1],["Clean, dry, tight linens",0]]},{"t":"check","q":"Which red area should you report to the nurse?","options":["A red area over the tailbone that does not fade","Pink cheeks after a warm bath","A red shirt","Lipstick"],"why":"Redness that does not fade can be the first stage of a pressure injury."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges check that the bed is locked and at a safe working height, that dirty linen never touches your uniform or the floor and that you never shake linens. They look for tight, smooth sheets with no wrinkles, a centered top sheet, a neat mitered corner and a 6-inch cuff. At the end they check that the pillow openings face away from the door, the bed is in its lowest position and the call light is on the bed. Working on one side at a time saves steps and helps you finish within 10 minutes. A neat, tidy bed earns points and protects skin."}]]
],takeaways:['Clean, smooth linens protect skin; roll dirty linen inward, hold it away from you and put it in the hamper—never shake it.','Lock and raise the bed, work one side at a time, fitted sheet tight, drawsheet in the center, top sheet wrong side up, mitered corner, 6-inch cuff.','Pillow open ends away from the door; lock wheels, lowest position, call light on the bed, safety check and hand hygiene.'],terms:[['Unoccupied bed','A bed with no patient in it.'],['Mitered corner','A neat folded corner that keeps linens tucked.'],['Drawsheet','A sheet across the middle of the bed used to move patients.']],mcqs:[q('How do you remove dirty linens?','Roll the edges inward','Shake them out','Pull them off quickly','Put them on the floor','Keep dirt away from your scrubs.'),q('Why lock the wheels?','So the bed does not move','To make it faster','To raise the bed','It does not matter','Safety first.'),q('How is the top sheet placed?','Wrong side up with the hem even with the top of the mattress','Right side up','Upside down at the foot','Folded in half','Seams face up.'),q('How big is the cuff?','6 inches','1 inch','12 inches','2 feet','Fold the top sheet over the blanket.'),q('Which way do the pillow openings face?','Away from the door','Toward the door','Toward the window','Up','It looks neat.'),q('Where do you put the call light?','On the bed','In a drawer','On the floor','On the windowsill','The patient must reach it.'),q('Why work on one side at a time?','It saves time and steps','It looks nicer','The judge likes it','It is not important','Finish one side first.'),q('How long do you have for Skill III?','10 minutes','3 minutes','6 minutes','20 minutes','Practice with a timer.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to make an unoccupied bed following HOSA Skill III.',rubric:[['Getting ready',['supplies','hand','order','lock','raise']],['Dirty linen',['roll','inward','hamper','one side','pillowcase']],['Making the bed',['fitted','drawsheet','center','wrong side up','hem','blanket','miter','6 inch']],['Finishing safely',['pillow','away from the door','lowest','call light','safety check','wash']]],model:'I gather supplies, wash my hands, put on gloves if needed and place the linens nearby in the order I will use them. I lock the bed wheels and raise the bed to a comfortable height. I remove the dirty linens by rolling the edges inward so they do not touch my scrubs, put them in the hamper and work on one side of the bed until it is finished. I take off the pillowcases and put them in the hamper. I put on the fitted bottom sheet, pulling the corners tight and smooth, and tuck the drawsheet tightly in the center. I unfold the top sheet wrong side up with the hem even with the top of the mattress, center the blanket, tuck both at the foot, make a mitered corner and fold a 6-inch cuff. I put the pillows in their cases with the open ends away from the door. Then I check that the wheels are locked, lower the bed to its lowest position, remove my gloves and wash my hands, place the call light on the bed, put things back, do a safety check and wash my hands again.'},sources:[RULES,AHRQ_PU]};

export const C5={title:'Making an occupied bed (Skill IV)',hook:'Sometimes a patient cannot get out of bed. You can still give them clean sheets by rolling the patient from side to side. Skill IV has 37 steps and 12 minutes. Keeping the patient safe, covered and comfortable is the most important part.',objectives:['Keep the patient safe and covered while changing linens.','Turn a patient safely using side rails.','Make an occupied bed following HOSA Skill IV.','Complete the safety checkpoints before leaving.'],sections:[
['Getting ready: steps 1–10',[
'**1.** Gather supplies. **2.** Wash your hands and put on gloves if needed. **3.** Greet the patient and say your name. **4.** Identify the patient. **5.** Explain what you will do. **6.** **Lock the wheels** and raise the bed to **hip level**. **7.** **Lower the side rail** on your working side (the other rail stays up). **8.** Put linens in the order you will use them. **9.** Take the **call light off the bed** so it does not get lost. **10.** Make the bed **flat** unless the care plan says otherwise.',
'Why keep one side rail up? When the patient rolls away from you, they hold the raised rail so they do not fall.',
tip('Before you start, tell the patient: “I am going to help you roll to your side so I can put clean sheets on. Please hold the rail.”')]],
['Covering the patient: steps 11–13',[
'**11.** Loosen the top linens from the foot of the bed. **12.** Put a **bath blanket** over the top linens. Ask the patient to **hold the top edge** of the bath blanket or tuck it under their shoulders. **13.** Pull the top linens out from under the bath blanket **without uncovering the patient**, and put them in the hamper. The bath blanket keeps the patient **warm and private**.',
chk('Why do you use a bath blanket?',['To keep the patient warm and covered while you change linens','To make the bed heavier','To clean the floor','To replace the pillow'],'Privacy and warmth matter.')]],
['First side: steps 14–19',[
'**14.** Help the patient **roll away from you** to the far side and ask them to **hold the side rail**. **15.** Move the pillow with the patient. **16.** Loosen the dirty bottom linens starting at the head of the bed and **tuck them against the patient’s back**. **17.** Place the clean **fitted sheet** with its center crease in the middle, pull the corners tight on your side and **roll the extra material toward the patient**. **18.** Place the **drawsheet**, tuck it in on your side and **fan-fold** the rest toward the middle. **19.** Tell the patient they will **roll over a bump of linen**, help them **turn toward you** and **raise the side rail**.',
order('First side',['Roll the patient away; patient holds the rail','Move the pillow with the patient','Tuck dirty linen against the patient’s back','Place the fitted sheet; roll extra toward the patient','Place and fan-fold the drawsheet','Roll the patient toward you over the linen; raise the rail'])]],
['Second side: steps 20–28',[
'**20.** Go to the other side and **lower that side rail**. **21.** Remove the dirty linen by **rolling it from the head to the foot** of the bed without touching your skin or clothes. **22.** Put it in the hamper. **23.** **Pull the clean linen through** quickly. **24.** Pull the fitted sheet tight and smooth over the corners. **25.** Pull the drawsheet into place and tuck it in **without wrinkles**. **26.** Help the patient turn to the **center of the bed on their back** and **raise the side rail**. **27.** Change the **pillowcases** and put the pillow under the patient’s head. **28.** Put the dirty pillowcases in the hamper.',
order('Second side',['Lower the rail on the other side','Roll dirty linen from head to foot into the hamper','Pull clean linen through','Tighten the fitted sheet and drawsheet','Turn the patient to the center; raise the rail','Change the pillowcase'])]],
['Top linens and checkpoints: steps 29–37',[
'**29.** Center the **top sheet** over the bath blanket using its center fold. **30.** Ask the patient to hold the top sheet, then **pull the bath blanket out** from underneath and put it in the hamper. **31.** Center the **blanket**, tuck the top sheet and blanket at the foot and make **mitered corners**. **32.** If needed, make **toe pleats**—a small fold at the foot—so the patient’s toes have room. **33.** Fold a **6-inch cuff**. **34.** Do the **checkpoints**: call light **within easy reach**, bed **lowered**, patient in **good body alignment** and report any changes to the nurse. **35.** Remove gloves and wash your hands. **36.** Talk kindly with the patient. **37.** Follow standard precautions.',
flip('Checkpoints before you leave',[['Call light','Within easy reach'],['Bed','Lowered to the lowest position'],['Body alignment','Patient straight and comfortable'],['Report','Tell the nurse about any changes'],['Side rails','As the care plan says'],['Hands','Remove gloves and wash hands']])]],
['Practice and safety check',[
kase('Safety first',{a:{text:'You are about to roll the patient away from you. The far side rail is down. What should you do?',choices:[['Raise the far side rail first so the patient can hold it','b','Correct. A raised rail keeps the patient from falling.',true],['Roll the patient anyway','b','The patient could fall off the bed.',false]]},b:{text:'You move to the other side of the bed. What must you do before you leave the first side?',choices:[['Raise the side rail on the first side','c','Correct. Never leave a rail down when you walk away.',true],['Leave it down','c','The patient could roll out.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish the bed, lower it, give the call light and report to the nurse. Well done!',end:true}}),
tip('Practice with a friend as the “patient.” Ask them to tell you if they ever felt uncovered or unsafe.')]],
["Positioning and comfort in bed",["Patients who stay in bed need to change position often for comfort and skin health. Common positions are: **supine** (lying flat on the back), **Fowler’s** (head of the bed raised about 45–60 degrees; **high Fowler’s** is about 90 degrees for eating), **lateral** (on the side), **Sims’** (on the left side with the top knee bent toward the chest) and **prone** (on the stomach). Pillows support the head, arms and legs so the body stays in **good alignment**—straight and comfortable.","Before you leave, check that the patient is comfortable, the body is lined up, the call light is in reach and the bed is low. Ask, “How does that feel?” Some patients cannot tell you they are uncomfortable, so watch their face and body for signs of pain such as frowning, moaning or restlessness, and tell the nurse.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the position","pairs":[["Supine","Flat on the back"],["Fowler’s","Head of bed raised about 45–60 degrees"],["High Fowler’s","Sitting almost straight up for meals"],["Lateral","Lying on the side"],["Sims’","Left side with the top knee bent"],["Prone","Lying on the stomach"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges watch patient safety most closely in Skill IV. They check that the side rail on the far side is up before you roll the patient away, that you raise a rail every time you walk to the other side and that the patient is never left uncovered. They listen for you to warn the patient about the “bump” of linen before they roll over it. At the end they check the call light, the bed height, the patient’s body alignment and your report to the nurse. Keep talking kindly to the patient throughout; the “patient” is often a volunteer."}]]
],takeaways:['Lock wheels, raise to hip level, lower only your working rail, flat bed, call light off the bed; cover with a bath blanket.','Roll the patient away (holding the rail), tuck dirty linen against their back, place clean linen and fan-fold the drawsheet, roll them toward you and raise the rail.','On the other side, roll dirty linen head to foot, pull clean linen through, finish the top linens with miters, toe pleats and a 6-inch cuff; checkpoints before you leave.'],terms:[['Occupied bed','A bed with a patient in it.'],['Bath blanket','A light blanket used to cover the patient during care.'],['Toe pleat','A small fold in the top linens to give the toes room.']],mcqs:[q('How high do you raise the bed for Skill IV?','To hip level','As high as it goes','It stays low','To shoulder level','Protects your back.'),q('What covers the patient while you remove the top linens?','A bath blanket','A towel','Nothing','The pillow','It keeps them warm and private.'),q('When the patient rolls away from you, what do they hold?','The side rail','Your arm','The pillow','The call light','The rail keeps them safe.'),q('How do you remove dirty bottom linen on the second side?','Roll it from head to foot','Pull it up toward the head','Shake it','Leave it in the bed','Keep it away from your clothes.'),q('What are toe pleats for?','To give the patient’s toes room','To make the bed look fancy','To hold the pillow','To cover the call light','A small fold at the foot.'),q('Which is a checkpoint before leaving?','Call light within easy reach','Turning off the TV','Opening the window','Taking the pillow away','Also lower the bed and check alignment.'),q('What do you do with the call light at the start?','Take it off the bed so it is not lost','Throw it away','Put it under the mattress','Give it to the nurse','Return it at the end.'),q('How long do you have for Skill IV?','12 minutes','3 minutes','6 minutes','30 minutes','Practice with a timer.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to make an occupied bed following HOSA Skill IV while keeping the patient safe and covered.',rubric:[['Getting ready',['greet','identify','explain','lock','hip level','side rail','flat']],['Covering and first side',['bath blanket','roll away','side rail','tuck','fitted','fan-fold']],['Second side',['toward you','raise','head to foot','pull','tight','center']],['Finishing and checkpoints',['pillowcase','top sheet','miter','toe','6 inch','call light','lower','alignment','report']]],model:'I gather supplies, wash my hands and put on gloves if needed, greet and identify the patient and explain what I will do. I lock the wheels, raise the bed to hip level, lower the side rail on my working side, put the linens in order, take the call light off the bed and make the bed flat. I loosen the top linens, cover the patient with a bath blanket and remove the top linens without uncovering them. I help the patient roll away from me and hold the side rail, move the pillow with them, tuck the dirty linen against their back, place the fitted sheet with its crease in the center and roll the extra toward them, and tuck and fan-fold the drawsheet. I help the patient roll toward me over the linen and raise the rail, go to the other side, lower that rail, roll the dirty linen from head to foot into the hamper, pull the clean linen through and pull it tight. I turn the patient to the center on their back, raise the rail, change the pillowcase, center the top sheet, remove the bath blanket, add the blanket, make mitered corners and toe pleats and fold a 6-inch cuff. Before leaving, I place the call light within reach, lower the bed, check good body alignment, report changes to the nurse, remove my gloves and wash my hands.'},sources:[RULES,AHRQ_PU,CDC_HH]};
